// 106 — Closures capturing local state — DESIGN NOTE
// A closure is a function value that carries a private copy of
// surrounding variables. JavaScript's classic counter:
//
//     function counter() { let n = 0; return () => ++n; }
//
// Each call to `counter()` produces an independent, encapsulated `n`.
// The runtime cost is heap-allocating the captured env per closure;
// the design cost is "where does my mutable state actually live?"
// being one extra hop away from the call site.
//
// AiLang has no closure type. The same encapsulation lives in a
// FixedPool — explicit storage with a name. To get N independent
// counters you declare N pools (or one pool with an array field
// indexed by counter id). The state is visible at the source level
// rather than implicit in the closure value.
FixedPool.Counter {
    "n": Initialize=0
}

Function.NextCount {
    Output: Integer
    Body: {
        Counter.n = Add(Counter.n, 1)
        ReturnValue(Counter.n)
    }
}

SubRoutine.Main {
    PrintMessage("count = ")
    PrintNumber(NextCount())
    PrintMessage("\ncount = ")
    PrintNumber(NextCount())
    PrintMessage("\ncount = ")
    PrintNumber(NextCount())
    PrintMessage("\n")
    PrintMessage("(state lives in FixedPool.Counter, not in a hidden closure env)\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
